WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Let’s talk about what’s going on in Hong Kong

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.080
because things are still very tense.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.680
Thousands of people have been protesting and occupying the streets

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.280
for months and parts of the city look like a war zone.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.480
What started out as an outcry over an extradition bill

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.240
reignited a pro-democracy movement that’s not backing down.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.520
So what exactly is Hong Kong’s ongoing stand-off about?

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.520
And where could it lead?

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.520
There’s a lot of history and background to the current unrest in Hong Kong.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.360
But the trigger was a high-profile murder case that exposed a legal loophole.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.240
In 2018, a couple from Hong Kong went on holiday to Taiwan.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.440
But 20-year-old Poon Hiu-wing never came home.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.560
A month later, her 19-year-old boyfriend Chan Tong-kai

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.880
confessed that he killed her.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.960
That’s Chan leaving his hotel in Taiwan 

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.240
with his girlfriend’s body allegedly inside that suitcase.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.280
Chan was arrested back in Hong Kong.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.320
But the problem was that Hong Kong’s courts couldn’t prosecute him

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.880
for a murder he said he committed in Taiwan.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.600
And officials in Taiwan said they couldn’t either

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.000
because there’s no extradition agreement between the two territories.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.440
So instead, Hong Kong’s authorities charged Chan with money laundering

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.200
because he used his girlfriend’s credit card after she died.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.760
because he used his girlfriend’s credit card after she died.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.080
but the question was, what to do about the murder case back in Taiwan?

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.040
So what Hong Kong lawmakers did, was propose a new extradition bill to allow 

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.640
suspects accused of serious crimes to face trial in Hong Kong or Taiwan

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.680
but also in China.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.080
And that rung alarm bells for many people.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.640
The Chinese government is often accused of going after its political opponents.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.120
Human Rights Watch says China’s dissidents have been subjected to show

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.040
trials, forced confessions and even torture.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.520
There have also been reports of people disappearing from Hong Kong

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.360
and ending up on the mainland.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.160
There’s a lot of arguments that centre around the Bookseller Case

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.600
a few years ago, where a bookseller in Hong Kong was being abducted 

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.480
to mainland China, and some other cases. So these fears, justified or not
 So these 

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.040
tells about the concerns that some of the people in Hong Kong that they 

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.080
do not believe in the mainland’s legal systems. 

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.760
So that’s why protesters have been so against the extradition bill.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.040
But there’s a deeper conflict at play here.  

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.920
And to explain that, we have to rewind a few years.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.040
Hong Kong was part of China for centuries. 

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.120
But after winning the first Opium War in the mid 1800s

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.680
 Britain ended up ruling Hong Kong for 150 years.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.800
It never became fully democratic, but Hong Kong did become a major

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.840
 business centre, while the Communist Party 

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.840
came into force on mainland China.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.200
“Hong Kong awaits the answer to the Communist army’s challenge.” 

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.360
 Then in 1997, Britain gave Hong Kong back.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.320
China will tonight take responsibility for a place

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.200
and a people which matter greatly to us all.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.760
But the two sides agreed that Hong Kong could keep

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.680
 some autonomy for 50 years

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.280
until it fully reunites with the mainland in 2047.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.120
So to soften the transition, they came up with a principle  

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.200
called “One Country, Two systems”.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.840
It means Hong Kong belongs to communist China 

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.000
but gets to run its own economy, police force, justice system

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.480
and it’s allowed some democratic reforms.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.880
We grew up learning that in Hong Kong we have freedom of speech

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.200
freedom of press, freedom of assembly. That we can check on the 

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:40.160
government whenever they do something that are not for the people. 

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.240
But China controls the bigger stuff

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.120
like the military, foreign affairs and to some extent, Hong Kong’s political system.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.320
For example, Hong Kong’s top political leader, the chief executive  

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.080
is selected by a closed committee and approved by China.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.840
Hong Kong’s law-making body, called the 

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.600
 legislative council, is also pretty unique.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.440
35 of its 70 seats are for elected politicians 

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.400
from both the pro-democracy and pro-China camps.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.640
But the other half is reserved for different sectors in Hong Kong

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.240
like education, health, tourism or finance.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.160
The issue with that system though

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.080
 is that many of those sectors have business ties with the mainland.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.360
So critics say that makes the pro-China bloc bigger, and leads to more 

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.760
pro-China laws being pushed through the legislative council.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.000
So there’s been this constant back and forth between those who feel part of

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.160
China and those who want to pull away.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:42.840
 Those who want more democracy, and those who don’t.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.040
And from time to time, the tension boils over.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.520
Over the years, Hong Kongers have taken to the streets to protest against

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.200
 all sorts of laws they’ve seen as stepping on their freedoms.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.080
In 2014, the student-led “Umbrella Movement” 

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.240
 shut down the city for 3 months. 

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.560
But this year’s demonstrations are different.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.240
These are the largest protests Hong Kong has ever seen

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.360
They’ve gone on for more than 6 months.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.440
And this time, the city’s pro-democracy movement has the support of many

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.200
more groups, including teachers and financial sector workers.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.160
There’s far more violence, too.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.120
Tear gas, rocks, molotov cocktails and even live bullets have been used.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.840
So far one person has died, dozens have been injured and hundreds have been

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.640
 charged with protest-related offences.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.360
Police and protesters blame each other for the increasingly intense confrontations.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.000
Parts of the city have been paralysed and badly damaged

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.800
 like transport systems and even the legislative council building.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.080
And there’s no sign of either side backing down.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.280
Protesters are also standing firm. They’ve got five main demands.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.680
One of them, was to withdraw the extradition bill. 

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.200
That happened in September.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.600
But they also want Hong Kong’s authorities to start 

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.760
a formal investigation into police violence, to release hundreds

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.680
of arrested protesters, for the police to stop labeling the protests as  “riots”

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:33.080
and they want universal suffrage, as in,  the freedom to elect all of Hong Kong’s

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.120
lawmakers plus their chief executive.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.400
Others are calling for something more radical:

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.160
 full independence from China

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.040
and that’s a red line for Beijing.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.280
In fact China’s President Xi Jinping has openly said 

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.840
 “Those who engage in separatist activities in any part of China  

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.840
 will be smashed into pieces.”

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.680
China’s state media even released videos of Chinese troops on the other side

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.160
side of the border, in case of a request from Hong Kong’s leaders, to intervene.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:08.400
But Hong Kongers weren’t swayed.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.800
And not only did they remain in the streets 

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.240
they sent Beijing a message from the polls.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.600
Recent local elections saw a record number of people vote

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:19.440
and the pro-democracy camp made big gains.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.800
 They now control 17 out of 18 councils.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.520
Before the election, the pro-Beijing camp had all 18.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.000
District council election is already the de facto referendum to prove

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.840
how Hong Kong people deserve free elections.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.800
As things stand, it’s really just a countdown to the year 2047.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.240
Some in Hong Kong see reunification with China as something inevitable.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.840
But others are clearly not going to give up Hong Kong’s autonomy

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.960
without putting up a real fight.

